## Chapter 1: The Problem — A Genome Can Contain Multiple Histories

A present-day genome is not necessarily the product of a single uninterrupted lineage. In sexually reproducing organisms, recombination partitions ancestral chromosomes into segments with potentially different evolutionary histories. Hybridization can introduce DNA from divergent lineages. Introgression can subsequently transfer genomic regions across species boundaries. Polyploidization preserves multiple homologous or homeologous chromosome sets. Incomplete lineage sorting produces gene trees that disagree with the species tree even without recent gene flow.

Consequently, at the level of individual genomic loci:

$$\text{one organism} \neq \text{one evolutionary history}$$

The Damask rose, *Rosa × damascena*, provides an exceptionally instructive case. It is a complex allotetraploid with a stable chromosome number of **2n = 4x = 28** (Gudin, 2000). This is the tetraploid cytotype: four haploid sets of seven chromosomes each, following the genus-wide base number x = 7. The Damask rose is generally described as having a probable triparental origin involving *R. moschata*, *R. gallica*, and *R. fedtschenkoana*. The 2026 *Rosa* pangenome has assembled 51 haplotypes from 23 accessions and identified 1,801,537 structural variants, revealing extensive introgression across sections.

This creates a precise scientific opportunity. The question is no longer whether the Damask rose is a hybrid—that is established. The question is:

> **Can local genomic histories be statistically distinguished in the polyploid genome of *Rosa × damascena*?**

This question is not merely descriptive. It is inferential. It asks whether the methodological tools exist to distinguish between processes that produce superficially similar genomic signatures: ancient hybridization followed by recombination, later introgression, incomplete lineage sorting, and ghost lineage introgression. Each process leaves a different spatial and phylogenetic footprint, but those footprints overlap in ways that make naive inference unreliable.

The central theoretical contribution of this thesis is:

$$\boxed{\textbf{Genomic history is distributed across the genome.}}$$

More precisely: when hybridization, introgression, polyploidy, and incomplete lineage sorting coexist, evolutionary history should be inferred as a distribution of local genomic histories rather than represented solely by a single species tree. The Damask rose is the test system. The scientific object is:

$$\mathcal{H}(x) = P(H_k \mid x, D)$$

where \(x\) is genomic position and \(H_k\) is an evolutionary-history class.

