## Chapter 5: Local Ancestry — The Ancestry-Block Framework

### 5.1 Genomic representation

Let the phased nuclear genome of *R. × damascena* be represented as:

$$G = \{g_1, g_2, \ldots, g_n\}$$

Partition the genome into windows:

$$W = \{w_1, w_2, \ldots, w_m\}$$

For each window \(w_i\), define an ancestry state:

$$z_i \in \{M, G, F, J, D, U\}$$

where \(M\) denotes *R. moschata*-like, \(G\) denotes *R. gallica*-like, \(F\) denotes *R. fedtschenkoana*-like, \(J\) denotes *R. majalis*-like, \(D\) denotes *R. davurica*-like, and \(U\) denotes unresolved. The primary objective is to estimate:

$$P(z_i = k \mid D)$$

where \(D\) represents the observed genomic data.

### 5.2 The mixture-model hidden Markov model

A standard hidden Markov model assumes a single, uniform process generating ancestry transitions across all genomic windows. This assumption is violated in *R. × damascena* because the tetraploid genome may exhibit both disomic and polysomic inheritance in different regions. We therefore propose a mixture-model hidden Markov model in which each genomic window is assigned a latent inheritance mode \(c_i \in \{\text{disomic}, \text{polysomic}\}\). The joint probability becomes:

$$P(Z, C, D) = P(z_1) \prod_{i=2}^{m} P(z_i \mid z_{i-1}, c_i) \prod_{i=1}^{m} P(c_i \mid c_{i-1}) \prod_{i=1}^{m} P(D_i \mid z_i, c_i)$$

where \(C = \{c_1, \ldots, c_m\}\) is the sequence of inheritance modes; \(P(z_i \mid z_{i-1}, c_i)\) is the ancestry transition probability conditional on inheritance mode; \(P(c_i \mid c_{i-1})\) models the spatial persistence of inheritance mode; and \(P(D_i \mid z_i, c_i)\) is the emission probability, which incorporates allele dosage expectations under each mode.

### 5.3 Reference-bias modeling

The emission probability must account for reference bias. We model this as:

$$P(D_i \mid z_i, c_i) = \sum_{r} P(D_i \mid z_i, c_i, \beta_r) P(\beta_r)$$

where \(\beta_r\) is a reference-bias parameter for candidate lineage \(r\). These parameters will be estimated externally using simulation: simulate reads from a high-quality genome of lineage \(r\) at various divergence levels from the reference, map them to the reference, and measure the mapping bias. The resulting function \(\beta_r(d)\) will be used as an informative prior, not estimated jointly with ancestry. This prevents bias parameters from absorbing ancestry signal.

