## Chapter 6: Evolutionary Scenario Discrimination

### 6.1 Scenarios, not models

Biologically, hybridization, introgression, incomplete lineage sorting, and ghost ancestry can coexist. It is therefore more accurate to define **evolutionary scenarios composed of processes** rather than artificially mutually exclusive models:

| Scenario | Description |
|---|---|
| \(S_1\) | Hybridization only |
| \(S_2\) | Hybridization + subsequent introgression |
| \(S_3\) | Incomplete lineage sorting-dominated |
| \(S_4\) | Hybridization + introgression + ghost ancestry |

ABC-RF will compare these scenarios. The scenarios are not mutually exclusive biological hypotheses; they are different **compositions of processes**. \(S_4\) includes hybridization, introgression, and ghost ancestry; \(S_2\) includes hybridization and introgression but not ghost ancestry. The comparison asks which composition of processes best explains the data, not which single process is the sole cause of all discordance.

### 6.2 ABC-random forest

For each scenario \(S_j\), we estimate \(P(D \mid S_j)\) using **Approximate Bayesian Computation with random forest** (ABC-RF). Classical rejection- or regression-ABC scales poorly with the high-dimensional summary vector required here. ABC-RF tolerates high-dimensional, possibly non-sufficient summary statistics much better and substantially cuts the simulation budget required for robustness sweeps. ABC-RF is robust to the inclusion of poorly informative or irrelevant summary statistics, making it well-suited to the complex scenario comparison required here.

**Pre-registered ABC-RF priors:**

| Parameter | Prior | Range |
|---|---|---|
| \(N_e\) (ancestral) | Log-uniform | \(10^4\)–\(10^5\) |
| \(t_{hybrid}\) | Log-uniform | 500–5,000 generations |
| \(t_{intro}\) | Log-uniform | 100–2,000 generations |
| \(r\) (recombination) | From LDhat/ReLERNN | Estimated |
| \(f_{clonal}\) | Uniform | 0.9–1.0 |

**Summary statistics** will include ancestry-block length distribution, topology weight distributions from Twisst, DFOIL and Δ-statistic values, \(F_{ST}\) between inferred ancestry blocks, site pattern \(f_d\), structural variant enrichment at ancestry boundaries, and linkage disequilibrium decay curves.

### 6.3 Directional introgression testing

The DFOIL method tests allelic patterns on the symmetric five-taxon tree \(S = (((1,2),(3,4)),5)\). More recent Δ-statistics generalize DFOIL and can incorporate or exclude singleton allelic patterns depending on taxon sampling. DFOIL has been shown to accurately detect ancestral introgression events and exhibits exceptionally low false-positive rates.

**Explicit methodological decision:** DFOIL/Δ-statistics will be run per-haplotype post-phasing where phasing quality permits. If phasing quality is insufficient, they will be run on a collapsed pseudo-diploid representation using the two most divergent haplotypes at each locus. A simulation-based power analysis will quantify expected power loss under the collapsed representation. We expect collapsed to lose power for recent introgression where introgressed haplotype frequency < 0.25.

### 6.4 Topology weighting

Twisst quantifies the contribution of each taxon topology to the full tree across genomic windows. Under incomplete lineage sorting alone, the three possible topologies for a trio of taxa should appear in approximately equal frequencies. Under introgression, one topology will be overrepresented. Twisst will be run on sub-genome-specific haplotype sets to determine whether introgression signals are confined to one sub-genome. As a phasing-free control, *twisst2* will be run on unphased genotype data of any ploidy.

