## Chapter 7: Robustness and Falsification

### 7.1 Data sufficiency precedes biological inference

$$\boxed{\text{No donor genome} \rightarrow \text{no ancestry claim.}}$$

This principle is operationalized through a Phase 0 audit that must be completed before any model specification begins. The audit documents, for each species, the assembly level (chromosome-level, contig-level, skim, or plastome-only), BUSCO completeness, contig N50, number of contigs, phasing method, switch error rate, and availability. Pre-specified decision rules determine whether each lineage passes the sampling sufficiency criterion (F6: at least one phased, chromosome-level assembly with BUSCO completeness > 90%).

The Phase 0 audit is not hypothetical. On current resources, the candidate set is already functionally down to three lineages. *R. moschata*, *R. gallica*, and *R. fedtschenkoana* are the only candidates with sufficient genomic resources to support ancestry inference. *R. majalis* fails F6: available resources are limited to Illumina genome skims (18.9 Gb of raw data from a single HiSeq 2500 run) and chloroplast sequences (157,258 bp); no chromosome-level assembly exists. *R. davurica* also fails F6: available resources are limited to a single chloroplast genome sequence (156 kb, accession MW381769) and resequencing data for genetic background analysis; no chromosome-level assembly exists.

*R. gallica* is the critical uncertainty. The tetraploid assembly is fragmented (>3,514 contigs) and the reported switch error rate for A13 is 3.27%. The pangenome reports BUSCO completeness of 94.2% for polyploid accessions and an average contig N50 of 29.2 Mb across assemblies, but the *R. gallica* assembly specifically has been flagged as problematic. Under the pre-registered F6 threshold (chromosome-level assembly, BUSCO > 90%), *R. gallica* is PROVISIONAL rather than PASS. If it fails F6 after further assembly refinement, the candidate set collapses to *R. moschata* and *R. fedtschenkoana*—a scenario that would require the thesis to formally test whether a bipartite model plus ghost introgression explains the data.

### 7.2 Falsification conditions

The project will be considered unsuccessful as a multi-lineage ancestry reconstruction if any of the following pre-registered conditions are met:

**F1 — Instability under methodological perturbation.** The inferred ancestry state for a given window must be consistent across >90% of bootstrap replicates and across at least three different reference genomes. Additionally, the window-level ancestry call must pass a chromosome-wide false discovery rate correction (Benjamini-Hochberg, q < 0.05) or a genome-wide Bonferroni correction.

**F2 — Incomplete lineage sorting explanation.** The signal of ancestry (e.g., DFOIL statistic with p < 0.01) must remain significant after simulating data under a pure incomplete lineage sorting model with parameters estimated from the data.

**F3 — Reference bias explanation.** Apparent donor-specific signals must not disappear when reads are simulated from each candidate donor genome and mapped to the reference under realistic error models.

**F4 — Structural variation support.** An ancestry block must show statistically significant enrichment (Fisher's exact test, p < 0.05 after multiple-testing correction) for at least one structural variant type compared to flanking regions after controlling for local repeat content, GC content, and recombination rate using ANCOVA, or must show a significant reduction in linkage disequilibrium decay inside the block compared to flanking regions after controlling for background recombination rate.

**F5 — Alternative scenarios.** The preferred scenario must have a Bayes factor of at least 3 (or equivalent ABC-RF posterior probability ratio > 0.66) over the next-best scenario. If violated, the thesis reports that the data cannot discriminate among scenarios, rather than choosing arbitrarily.

**F6 — Sampling sufficiency.** Candidate donor species must be represented by at least one phased, chromosome-level assembly with BUSCO completeness > 90%.

**F7 — Identifiability.** The mixture-model hidden Markov model must recover known \((z, c, \beta)\) parameters from simulated data with accuracy above pre-specified thresholds (>80% correct ancestry assignment at 10× coverage; <20% inheritance-mode confusion; \(\beta_r\) correlation >0.7).

**F8 — Ghost lineage explanation.** The signal of introgression must remain significant after simulating data under a model with a proximal ghost lineage that contributes to both *R. × damascena* and one candidate donor.

### 7.3 Negative control calibration

A known diploid hybrid from the pangenome (e.g., *R. chinensis* × *R. odorata*) will be run through the full pipeline as a negative control. The false-positive rate for *R. moschata*/*R. gallica*/*R. fedtschenkoana* calls will be reported and used to calibrate F1–F8 thresholds. If the negative control produces a false-positive ancestry call at a rate exceeding 10%, the thresholds will be adjusted upward before applying the pipeline to *R. × damascena*.

