## Chapter 10: Generalization to Other Polyploid Hybrid Species

Many plants have evolutionary histories involving hybridization, polyploidy, introgression, and selection. A framework capable of separating these processes could be applied to other domesticated and polyploid plants, including wheat, potato, cotton, and strawberry.

The reusable pipeline—**mixture-HMM + ABC-RF + DFOIL/Δ + Twisst + twisst2/sticcs + falsification gate**—is itself a portable contribution to other polyploid systems. The pipeline will be released as an open-source, reproducible workflow on GitHub, implemented as a Nextflow or Snakemake pipeline with containerization. All intermediate data and the ancestry map will be deposited. The F1–F8 falsification criteria will be pre-registered on OSF before Phase III begins.

### 10.1 The broader conceptual contribution

The Damask rose serves as a model problem for a broader question:

> **How can evolutionary history be reconstructed when different regions of a genome contain different histories?**

This reframes phylogenomics from the search for a single tree toward the reconstruction of a distributed genomic history, with explicit acknowledgment of where inference succeeds and where it fails. The central theoretical contribution is the demonstration that when reticulate processes are substantial, genomic history is better represented as a spatially distributed inference problem than as a single tree.

### 10.2 Adversarial collaboration

To further strengthen the falsification framework, an adversarial collaboration is proposed with a researcher skeptical of the triparental hypothesis. The collaborator will independently analyze the data using their preferred methods (e.g., purely phylogenetic approaches, demographic inference with MSMC or fastsimcoal2). The goal is to see whether different methods converge on the same ancestry map. If they do not, the disagreement itself is a valuable finding. The collaborator will be selected based on expertise in population genetics, phylogenetics, or polyploid evolution, and will commit 10–20% FTE for 12 months. Products will include a co-authored preprint reporting convergent or divergent results.

### 10.3 Downstream biological validation (optional)

Exploratory analyses linking ancestry to phenotype—for example, testing whether *R. fedtschenkoana*-like ancestry is enriched near flowering-time genes such as *CCD4* or *TFL1*—will be conducted as downstream validation if time permits. These analyses will not be part of the primary hypothesis and will not affect the core inference framework. They represent a potential second paper rather than a component of the PhD's central contribution.

