## Conclusion

The Damask rose provides a particularly suitable system for studying genomic evolution because multiple independent lines of evidence point toward a complex history involving polyploidy and contributions from several *Rosa* lineages. It is a tetraploid with 2n = 4x = 28, a segmental allotetraploid with stable chromosome number. The 2026 pangenome now provides a substantially richer genomic basis for testing that history.

The proposed PhD focuses on one precise unresolved problem:

$$\boxed{\textbf{Can local genomic histories be statistically distinguished in the polyploid genome of }Rosa\times damascena?}$$

The answer should emerge not from a predetermined ancestry narrative but from the convergence—or failure of convergence—of independent genomic evidence, evaluated against pre-registered quantitative falsification criteria.

The ultimate object of reconstruction is not merely a phylogenetic tree. It is:

$$\boxed{\text{a probabilistic, haplotype-resolved map of evolutionary history across the genome, with explicit uncertainty and falsification status.}}$$

The core theoretical contribution is the demonstration that when reticulate processes are substantial, genomic history is better represented as a spatially distributed inference problem than as a single tree. The Damask rose is the test case. The framework is the contribution.

