## Appendix B: F7 Identifiability Simulation Design

### B.1 Purpose

This appendix specifies the simulation design for the identifiability check (F7) described in Chapter 5.3. The goal is to determine whether the mixture-model HMM can recover known ancestry (\(z\)), inheritance mode (\(c\)), and reference-bias (\(\beta\)) parameters under realistic data quality conditions.

### B.2 Simulation parameters

| Parameter | Values simulated | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Number of donors | 2, 3, 5 | Test model complexity |
| Coverage per donor | 5×, 10×, 20×, 40× | Realistic range |
| Divergence from reference | 0%, 1%, 3%, 5% | Calibrate \(\beta_r(d)\) |
| True ancestry blocks | 50, 100, 200 | Genome-wide scale |
| Block length distribution | Exponential, mean 5 Mb | Realistic |
| Inheritance mode | Disomic, polysomic, mixed | Test \(c_i\) recovery |
| Switch error rate | 0%, 1%, 2%, 5% | Test phasing sensitivity |
| Reference bias \(\beta_r\) | 0.1, 0.3, 0.5 | Test bias recovery |

### B.3 Simulation workflow

```
For each parameter combination:
  1. Simulate ancestral genomes for each donor
  2. Simulate R. × damascena as mosaic of donor haplotypes
  3. Apply inheritance mode (disomic/polysomic) per block
  4. Generate reads at specified coverage
  5. Introduce reference bias at specified level
  6. Apply phasing with specified SER
  7. Run mixture-model HMM
  8. Compare inferred (z, c, β) to true values
  9. Record confusion matrix and parameter correlations
```

### B.4 Pre-registered performance thresholds

| Metric | Threshold | Action if violated |
|---|---|---|
| Ancestry accuracy (10×) | > 80% | Downgrade donor to "unresolved" |
| Ancestry accuracy (5×) | > 60% | Report as provisional |
| Inheritance-mode confusion | < 20% | Fix \(c_i\) from cytogenetics |
| \(\beta_r\) correlation | > 0.7 | Use externally calibrated prior |
| Block boundary accuracy | > 70% | Increase window size or simplify |
| Convergence (Gelman-Rubin) | < 1.1 | Increase MCMC iterations |

### B.5 Expected confusion matrix format

|  | Inferred M | Inferred G | Inferred F | Inferred U |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **True M** | \(a_{MM}\) | \(a_{MG}\) | \(a_{MF}\) | \(a_{MU}\) |
| **True G** | \(a_{GM}\) | \(a_{GG}\) | \(a_{GF}\) | \(a_{GU}\) |
| **True F** | \(a_{FM}\) | \(a_{FG}\) | \(a_{FF}\) | \(a_{FU}\) |
| **True U** | \(a_{UM}\) | \(a_{UG}\) | \(a_{UF}\) | \(a_{UU}\) |

**Diagonal dominance** (\(a_{MM}, a_{GG}, a_{FF} > 0.8\)) is required for confident ancestry assignment. Off-diagonal elements quantify the rate at which one donor is misclassified as another.

### B.6 Reference-bias calibration

Reference bias parameters will be estimated externally by simulating reads from high-quality genomes of each lineage at divergence levels of 0%, 1%, 3%, and 5% from the reference. Mapping bias will be measured as the proportion of reads that fail to map or map incorrectly. The resulting function \(\beta_r(d)\) will be used as an informative prior in the HMM, not estimated jointly with ancestry. This prevents bias parameters from absorbing ancestry signal.

### B.7 Reporting

Results will be reported as:

1. **Confusion matrices** for each parameter combination
2. **Parameter recovery plots** (inferred vs. true)
3. **Power curves** (accuracy vs. coverage)
4. **Recommendation** for minimum coverage per donor
5. **Decision** on whether to proceed with full model or simplified model

